From 0004000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: GameSpark <gamespark@localhost>
Date: Sat, 17 Oct 2026 22:30:00 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 4/4] mtrack: code pages made writable check their own code (FEX_GSSMCWX)

Local GameSpark patch, not submitted upstream (FEX does not accept AI-generated code). Enabled with FEX_GSSMCWX=1;
off by default, and only active with SMCChecks=mtrack (FEX's default).

Street Fighter 6's Denuvo hooks ntdll's NtProtectVirtualMemory stub with kernelbase's WriteProcessMemory: the page is
made PAGE_EXECUTE_WRITECOPY (host RWX), NtWriteVirtualMemory writes the hook bytes, the old protection is restored.
Wine always performs NtWriteVirtualMemory in wineserver, with process_vm_writev. Under mtrack FEX write-protects a
writable page (PROT_READ) as soon as it translates code on it, and the stub's own return path is translated right
after the mprotect, so process_vm_writev fails with EFAULT: WriteProcessMemory returns ERROR_PARTIAL_COPY, Denuvo
raises "Error at hooking API NtProtectVirtualMemory" and exits with 0xDEADC0DE. SMCChecks=full never write-protects,
so the game boots with it, but checking every instruction of the whole program costs a lot (CPU-bound at ~48 fps in
Training Mode at 4K).

With FEX_GSSMCWX=1, an mprotect that makes executable pages writable marks them (an interval list in the syscall
handler, guarded by the VMA tracking lock) until an mprotect makes them non-writable again or they are unmapped:
- mtrack does not write-protect marked pages, so writes from other processes land, as on Windows;
- the frontend asks the syscall handler (new FEXCore::HLE::SyscallHandler::NeedsFullSMCChecks) about each decoded
  block and sets ForceFullSMCDetection on blocks with code on a marked page: each of their instructions compares its
  bytes before it runs, whichever way the block is entered (dispatcher, block link, call-return stack);
- GuestMprotect already invalidates the mprotected range after tracking it, so code translated before the mark is
  dropped and translated again with the checks, and the mprotect that unmarks the pages invalidates them again.
FEX_GSSMCWX=2 also marks every page mapped or mprotected writable+executable, whatever it was before (SF6's
executable has a 318 MB RWX section): slower, for memory where mtrack misses writes (it also misses writes to a RWX
page while another thread runs code on that page). FEX_GSSMCWX_LOG=1 logs the marks to stderr. Not for use with FEX's
disk cache: blocks loaded from it skip the frontend.
